Today I did a coding challenge at codewar.com.
I also completed a third challenge:
Define a function that takes an integer argument and returns a logical value True or False 
depending on if the integer is a prime.
A valid prime number:
 -Is a natural number greater than 1.
 -Has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself.
 -Must be handled efficiently to avoid timeouts for numbers up to 2^31.

First, I handled edge cases by checking if the number was less than 2. 
To optimize performance, I declared a variable called limit to store the square root of the number, 
as any divisor larger than the square root would have a corresponding partner smaller than it.
Then, I used a loop to check if the number is divisible by any integer from 2 up to this limit using the modulo operator. 
If a divisor is found, the function returns False. If the loop completes without finding any other divisors, the function returns True.
